// pktsum.nv — the internet checksum, the pseudo-headers it is taken
// over, and a verdict with four cases rather than a boolean.
//
// THE ARITHMETIC
//
// RFC 1071 defines one checksum and every protocol here uses it: the
// sixteen-bit one's-complement of the one's-complement sum of the
// bytes, taken two at a time, big-endian, with a zero byte appended if
// the length is odd.  The carries fold back in, which is what makes
// the sum independent of the order the words are added in and of where
// the odd byte falls.
//
// The field being checked is included in the sum as zero when it is
// computed, so a receiver can sum the whole header INCLUDING the
// checksum field and expect 0xFFFF.  Both forms are here:
// `checksum_of` computes the value to write, and comparing it with the
// carried one is what `compare` does.
//
// THE PSEUDO-HEADER, AND WHY IT IS BYTES
//
// TCP, UDP and ICMPv6 do not checksum only their own bytes.  RFC 9293
// section 3.1 and RFC 768 prepend a twelve-byte pseudo-header — source
// address, destination address, a zero, the protocol, and the
// upper-layer length — and RFC 8200 section 8.1 prepends a forty-byte
// one for IPv6.  It is not transmitted; it exists so that a datagram
// delivered to the wrong host or the wrong protocol fails its
// checksum.
//
// `PktPseudo` holds those bytes rather than the addresses, and that is
// deliberate.  A struct with two `Ipv4` fields and a struct with two
// `Ipv6` fields would be two types, and every TCP and UDP function
// that verifies a checksum would need two spellings.  The
// specification defines the pseudo-header as a byte layout, the sum
// consumes bytes, and one type with two constructors keeps the version
// difference where it belongs — at the point where the caller knows
// which IP header it came from.  Holding the addresses instead would
// also make the struct `@value`, because they are, and a `@value`
// struct cannot come back from a `Result` (`E2015`).
//
// THE IPv6 LENGTH IS NOT THE PAYLOAD LENGTH
//
// The pseudo-header's length field is the UPPER-LAYER packet length.
// For IPv4 that is the total length minus the header length.  For IPv6
// it is the payload length minus every extension header in the chain,
// and a datagram with a routing header and a destination-options
// header has two subtractions to make.  A verifier that used the
// payload length directly reports a wrong checksum on every IPv6
// datagram with an extension header, which is the commonest false
// alarm in IPv6 analysis.  `pktipv6.pseudo` does the subtraction from
// the walked chain.
//
// FOUR ANSWERS, NOT TWO
//
// `PktChecksum` has four cases because a boolean has to lie about two
// of them.  A UDP datagram over IPv4 with a zero checksum has NOT
// failed its checksum; RFC 768 says a zero means the sender did not
// compute one, and reporting that as corruption is wrong.  A packet
// whose covered bytes were cut off by the capture's snapshot length
// cannot be checked at all, and neither can an outgoing packet
// captured before the network card filled its checksum in — which is
// what a local capture of an offloaded send looks like, and why
// tcpdump on a sender's own machine shows a wall of bad checksums that
// are not bad.

// The verdict on a checksum.
//
// Four cases, because "valid" and "wrong" cannot describe a checksum
// nobody computed and cannot describe one the reader has not got the
// bytes to check.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(pktsum.checksum_reason(PktChecksumAbsent))
// ```
pub enum PktChecksum
    // The computed value matches the one the packet carries.
    PktChecksumValid
    // The computed value does not match.  `want` is what the bytes
    // say it should be and `got` is what the packet carried, so a log
    // line can print both.
    PktChecksumWrong(want: Int, got: Int)
    // The packet carries a zero checksum where zero means "not
    // computed".  RFC 768 allows it for UDP over IPv4 and only there:
    // a zero TCP checksum is a wrong checksum, and RFC 8200 section
    // 8.1 requires a UDP checksum over IPv6, with the tunnelling
    // exception of RFC 6935 the only way one is legally absent.
    // This is NOT valid and NOT wrong, and `is_valid` answers false.
    PktChecksumAbsent
    // The bytes the checksum covers are not all available, so no
    // verdict is possible.  `why` says which: the capture's snapshot
    // length cut the packet short, or the packet was taken from the
    // sending host before the network card computed the checksum.
    // Reporting this as wrong is how a capture taken on a sender's own
    // machine appears to be full of corruption.
    PktChecksumUnverifiable(why: Str)

// The twelve or forty bytes a transport checksum is taken over before
// the transport header itself.
//
// Never transmitted.  It exists so that a datagram delivered to the
// wrong address or the wrong protocol fails its checksum, which is the
// only end-to-end check either of those gets.
//
// IT HOLDS THE BYTES AND NOT THE ADDRESSES, for two reasons.  A struct
// with two `Ipv4` fields and a struct with two `Ipv6` fields would be
// two types, and every function here that verifies a checksum would
// need two spellings of itself.  And ipaddr-nv's addresses are `@value`
// structs, so a struct holding them is `@value` too (`E2015`) and could
// not come back from a `Result` — which `pktipv4.pseudo` and
// `pktipv6.pseudo` both do, because computing the upper-layer length
// can fail.  The specification defines the pseudo-header as a byte
// layout and the sum consumes bytes, so holding the bytes is also the
// closer reading.
//
// ```novo
// use ip
// use pktsum
// use pktproto
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = pktsum.pseudo_ipv4(ip.ipv4(10, 0, 0, 1), ip.ipv4(10, 0, 0, 2),
//                                PktProtoUdp, 12)
//     println("${pktsum.pseudo_length(p)}")
// ```
pub struct PktPseudo
    // The pseudo-header exactly as it is summed: twelve bytes for the
    // IPv4 form of RFC 9293 section 3.1, forty for the IPv6 form of
    // RFC 8200 section 8.1.
    header: Bytes
    // The upper-layer packet length the pseudo-header carries: the
    // transport header plus its payload, and nothing of the IP header.
    length: Int
    // The protocol number the pseudo-header carries — 6, 17 or 58.
    protocol: Int
    // Whether this is the forty-byte IPv6 form.
    over_ipv6: Bool

// The twelve-byte IPv4 pseudo-header (RFC 9293 section 3.1).
//
// `length` is the upper-layer packet length: the TCP or UDP header
// plus its payload, which for a parsed datagram is the IP total length
// minus the IP header length.  It is NOT the IP total length, and
// using that is a checksum that is wrong by exactly the header length.
//
// ```novo
// use ip
// use pktsum
// use pktproto
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = pktsum.pseudo_ipv4(ip.ipv4(192, 0, 2, 1), ip.ipv4(192, 0, 2, 2),
//                                PktProtoTcp, 40)
//     println("${p.length}")
// ```
pub fn pseudo_ipv4(source: Ipv4, destination: Ipv4, protocol: PktProtocol, length: Int) -> PktPseudo
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.pseudo_ipv4")

// The forty-byte IPv6 pseudo-header (RFC 8200 section 8.1).
//
// `next_header` is the UPPER-LAYER protocol — 6, 17 or 58 — and not
// the next-header value out of the IPv6 header, which for a datagram
// with an extension chain names the first extension header instead.
// A verifier that copied the IPv6 header's own field computes the sum
// over 0 or 44 and reports every such datagram as corrupt.
//
// `length` is the upper-layer packet length: the payload length less
// every extension header. `pktipv6.pseudo` computes both from a walked
// chain.
//
// ```novo
// use ip
// use pktsum
// use pktproto
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = pktsum.pseudo_ipv6(ip.ipv6_zero(), ip.ipv6_zero(), PktProtoIcmpv6, 8)
//     println("${p.over_ipv6}")
// ```
pub fn pseudo_ipv6(source: Ipv6, destination: Ipv6, next_header: PktProtocol,
                   length: Int) -> PktPseudo
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.pseudo_ipv6")

// The pseudo-header as the bytes the sum runs over: twelve for IPv4,
// forty for IPv6.
//
// For a caller checksumming with something else — a hardware offload
// path, a test comparing against a reference implementation — and for
// showing what was summed when a verdict is disputed.  It allocates.
//
// ```novo
// use std.bytes
// use ip
// use pktsum
// use pktproto
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = pktsum.pseudo_ipv4(ip.ipv4_zero(), ip.ipv4_zero(), PktProtoUdp, 8)
//     println(bytes.to_hex(pktsum.pseudo_bytes(p)))
// ```
pub fn pseudo_bytes(p: PktPseudo) -> Bytes
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.pseudo_bytes")

// The upper-layer packet length the pseudo-header carries.
//
// ```novo
// use ip
// use pktsum
// use pktproto
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${pktsum.pseudo_length(pktsum.pseudo_ipv4(ip.ipv4_zero(), ip.ipv4_zero(), PktProtoTcp, 20))}")
// ```
pub fn pseudo_length(p: PktPseudo) -> Int
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.pseudo_length")

// The one's-complement sum of a span, before folding and before
// complementing.
//
// The raw accumulator of RFC 1071 section 1.  Exposed because a caller
// summing several regions — a pseudo-header, a header, a payload held
// in a different buffer — adds the partial sums and folds once at the
// end, and folding each part separately would be wasted work.  An odd
// length is padded with one zero byte, as the RFC requires.
//
// ```novo
// use std.bytes
// use pktspan
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match pktsum.ones_complement_sum(bytes.zeros(20), pktspan.span(0, 20))
//         Ok(v)  => println("${v}")
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn ones_complement_sum(buf: Bytes, s: PktSpan) -> Result<Int, PktError>
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.ones_complement_sum")

// Fold a raw sum's carries back in and complement it, giving the
// sixteen-bit value that goes in the field.
//
// The second half of RFC 1071 section 1: add the high sixteen bits to
// the low sixteen until nothing is left above bit fifteen, then
// complement.  A caller that summed several regions with
// `ones_complement_sum` calls this once.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${pktsum.ones_complement_fold(0x1FFFF)}")
// ```
pub fn ones_complement_fold(sum: Int) -> Int
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.ones_complement_fold")

// The checksum of a span, with no pseudo-header.
//
// What the IPv4 header checksum (RFC 791 section 3.1) and the ICMP for
// IPv4 checksum (RFC 792) are: the sum over the bytes themselves and
// nothing else.  The caller zeroes the checksum field first, or
// subtracts it, because the field is part of what is summed.
//
// ```novo
// use std.bytes
// use pktspan
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match pktsum.checksum_of(bytes.zeros(20), pktspan.span(0, 20))
//         Ok(v)  => println("${v}")
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn checksum_of(buf: Bytes, s: PktSpan) -> Result<Int, PktError>
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.checksum_of")

// The checksum of a span with a pseudo-header prepended.
//
// What the TCP checksum, the UDP checksum and the ICMPv6 checksum are.
// The span is the transport header and its payload; the pseudo-header
// is summed first and is not part of the span.
//
// ```novo
// use std.bytes
// use ip
// use pktspan
// use pktsum
// use pktproto
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let p = pktsum.pseudo_ipv4(ip.ipv4_zero(), ip.ipv4_zero(), PktProtoUdp, 8)
//     match pktsum.checksum_with_pseudo(bytes.zeros(64), pktspan.span(34, 8), p)
//         Ok(v)  => println("${v}")
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn checksum_with_pseudo(buf: Bytes, s: PktSpan, p: PktPseudo) -> Result<Int, PktError>
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.checksum_with_pseudo")

// The verdict for a protocol where a zero checksum means zero.
//
// `PktChecksumValid` when the two agree and `PktChecksumWrong`
// otherwise, with no third case: for IPv4 headers, for ICMP, for
// ICMPv6 and for TCP, a carried zero is a value like any other and
// almost certainly a wrong one.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(pktsum.checksum_reason(pktsum.compare(0x1234, 0x1234)))
//     println(pktsum.checksum_reason(pktsum.compare(0x1234, 0x0000)))
// ```
pub fn compare(computed: Int, carried: Int) -> PktChecksum
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.compare")

// The verdict for UDP over IPv4, where a carried zero means the sender
// did not compute one.
//
// `PktChecksumAbsent` when `carried` is zero, and otherwise what
// `compare` answers.  RFC 768 makes the UDP checksum optional over
// IPv4, and a great deal of real traffic — some DNS, most tunnelling
// protocols, a lot of embedded telemetry — sends zero.  A tool using
// `compare` here counts all of it as corrupt.
//
// There is deliberately no UDP-over-IPv6 version: RFC 8200 section 8.1
// requires the checksum there, so a zero is wrong, and `compare` is
// the right function.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(pktsum.checksum_reason(pktsum.compare_udp_ipv4(0x1234, 0x0000)))
// ```
pub fn compare_udp_ipv4(computed: Int, carried: Int) -> PktChecksum
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.compare_udp_ipv4")

// The verdict when the covered bytes are not all present.
//
// `why` is the reason, for a status column: the capture's snapshot
// length, or a checksum the sending host's network card had not filled
// in yet.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(pktsum.checksum_reason(pktsum.unverifiable("snapshot length")))
// ```
pub fn unverifiable(why: Str) -> PktChecksum
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.unverifiable")

// Whether the verdict is `PktChecksumValid`.
//
// FALSE for `PktChecksumAbsent` and for `PktChecksumUnverifiable`, and
// that is the point: `not is_valid(c)` does NOT mean the packet is
// corrupt.  A tool counting bad checksums matches
// `PktChecksumWrong` and nothing else; a tool deciding whether to
// trust a payload treats absent and unverifiable as "unknown", which
// is what they are.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${pktsum.is_valid(PktChecksumValid)}")
//     println("${pktsum.is_valid(PktChecksumAbsent)}")
// ```
pub fn is_valid(c: PktChecksum) -> Bool
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.is_valid")

// A one-line description of the verdict, for a display.
//
// `"ok"`, `"wrong: computed 0x1234, carried 0x0000"`, `"not computed
// by the sender"`, `"unverifiable: snapshot length"`.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(pktsum.checksum_reason(PktChecksumWrong(0x1234, 0)))
// ```
pub fn checksum_reason(c: PktChecksum) -> Str
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.checksum_reason")

// A short stable code for a counter: `"valid"`, `"wrong"`,
// `"absent"`, `"unverifiable"`.
//
// Carries nothing the packet chose, so it is safe as a metric label.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println(pktsum.checksum_code(PktChecksumUnverifiable("offload")))
// ```
pub fn checksum_code(c: PktChecksum) -> Str
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.checksum_code")

// The value a UDP sender writes when the computed checksum is zero.
//
// 0xFFFF, because zero in the field means "no checksum" and a sender
// that wrote the computed zero would be telling the receiver not to
// check.  The two are equal in one's-complement arithmetic, so writing
// 0xFFFF changes nothing about the check and everything about what the
// field means.  RFC 768 states it in the UDP description, and it
// applies to UDP alone: a TCP checksum that computes to zero is
// written as zero.
//
// Answers its argument unchanged for every other value.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${pktsum.udp_transmit_value(0)}")
//     println("${pktsum.udp_transmit_value(0x1234)}")
// ```
pub fn udp_transmit_value(computed: Int) -> Int
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.udp_transmit_value")

// Whether a carried zero means "not computed" for this protocol.
//
// True only for UDP over IPv4.  The rule as a function, so a generic
// verifier that has a `PktProtocol` and a version can choose between
// `compare` and `compare_udp_ipv4` without writing the condition out
// again in every tool.
//
// ```novo
// use pktproto
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${pktsum.zero_means_absent(PktProtoUdp, false)}")
//     println("${pktsum.zero_means_absent(PktProtoUdp, true)}")
// ```
pub fn zero_means_absent(p: PktProtocol, over_ipv6: Bool) -> Bool
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.zero_means_absent")

// A checksum updated for a field that changed, without re-summing the
// packet.
//
// RFC 1624 section 3's equation.  A router decrementing a TTL, a
// network address translator rewriting an address and a port, a test
// harness mutating one field: each changes a couple of bytes, and
// re-summing a nine-thousand-byte datagram to reflect two of them is
// the difference between a router that forwards at line rate and one
// that does not.
//
// `old` and `new` are the sixteen-bit words that changed.  The earlier
// equation in RFC 1141 is subtly wrong — it can produce 0x0000 where
// 0xFFFF is meant, which in UDP means "no checksum" — and RFC 1624
// exists to correct it. This function is the corrected form.
//
// ```novo
// use pktsum
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${pktsum.incremental_update(0xB861, 0x4000, 0x0000)}")
// ```
pub fn incremental_update(checksum: Int, old: Int, new: Int) -> Int
    todo("packet-nv.pktsum.incremental_update")
